      ⍝ Bugs28 #52 regression: the PARENTHESIZED selective-specification
      ⍝ form (V[])←B (unlike the plain, unparenthesized V[]←B just above,
      ⍝ which was already correct) silently replaced V wholesale with B,
      ⍝ with none of the shape conformance an ordinary selective
      ⍝ specification requires -- (V[])←1 2 3 turned V←⍳5 into the
      ⍝ 3-element vector 1 2 3 instead of requiring ⍴,B to be 1 or 5.
      ⍝ Root cause: Prefix.cc's A[] (elided-index) handling built its
      ⍝ result via Z=CLONE(A,LOC) -- with the active NEW_CLONE scheme,
      ⍝ CLONE() is not a real copy, just a new Value_P handle on the
      ⍝ SAME Value object -- so when A was an lval-cellref array
      ⍝ carrying Symbol::resolve_lv()'s set_lval_whole_symbol() marker
      ⍝ (normally only meant for a genuinely EMPTY selector, e.g.
      ⍝ ((⍳0)⊃sym)←B, to mean "replace the whole array, no conformance
      ⍝ check"), that marker survived onto Z completely unchanged, even
      ⍝ though A[] selects EVERY position of A, not none of them. Fixed
      ⍝ by explicitly clearing the marker on Z, since CLONE() doesn't
      ⍝ actually build the fresh Value every other lvalue-producing
      ⍝ function is documented to return.
      ⍝
      V←⍳5
      (V[])←1 2 3
LENGTH ERROR+
      (V[])←1 2 3
      ^    ^

      )SIC
      ⍴V
5

      V←⍳5
      (V[])←1 2 3 4 5
      V
1 2 3 4 5

      ⍝ control: a genuinely empty selector still replaces wholesale
      ⍝
      V←⍳5
      (V[⍬])←1 2 3
      ⍴V
5

      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs31 #18): a 1-ELEMENT non-scalar
      ⍝ RHS (e.g. ,9, shape ,1) used to be enclosed into a scalar
      ⍝ selection instead of scalar-extended -- Value::assign_cellrefs()
      ⍝ checked new_value->is_scalar() (rank == 0) instead of ⍴,new_value
      ⍝ being 1, contradicting LRM p.43 rule 2 ("an array with empty
      ⍝ shape when ones are removed" is exempt from rule 1's enclosure
      ⍝ just as much as a genuine scalar). Every one of these must give
      ⍝ the plain scalar 9, matching (…)←9.
      ⍝
      A←5 ⋄ (A[])←,9 ⋄ A ⋄ ≡A
9
0

      M←2 2⍴⍳4 ⋄ (M[1;1])←,9 ⋄ M[1;1] ⋄ ≡M[1;1]
9
0

      ⍝ control: the ordinary (non-selective, already-correct) forms
      ⍝
      V←1 2 3 ⋄ (V[1])←,9 ⋄ V ⋄ ≡V
9 2 3
1

      V←1 2 3 ⋄ (V[1])←9 ⋄ V
9 2 3

      V←1 2 3 ⋄ (1↑V)←,9 ⋄ V
9 2 3

      V←1 2 3 ⋄ (V[1 2])←,9 ⋄ V
9 9 3

      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs31 #19): the identity fast paths
      ⍝ of ⌽ (scalar), 0⌽/0⊖, ⍉ (scalar/vector, and identity dyadic ⍉)
      ⍝ return their argument ALIASED (CLONE() under the active
      ⍝ NEW_CLONE scheme is not a real copy, just a new Value_P handle
      ⍝ on the SAME Value object), so Symbol::resolve_lv()'s
      ⍝ set_lval_whole_symbol() marker survived the "selecting" function
      ⍝ unbroken -- Value::assign_cellrefs() then wrongly took its
      ⍝ wholesale-replace shortcut (skipping all shape conformance)
      ⍝ instead of an ordinary selective specification, e.g. (0⌽V)←9
      ⍝ silently replaced V wholesale with the scalar 9 instead of
      ⍝ scalar-extending it to 9 9 9. Fixed by Value::clear_lval_markers(),
      ⍝ called at each aliasing identity fast path (see
      ⍝ Bif_F12_ROTATE.cc/Bif_F12_TRANSPOSE.cc/Bif_F12_TAKE_DROP.cc/
      ⍝ Bif_F12_COMMA.cc) -- same root cause as Bugs28 #52 (elided A[]),
      ⍝ generalized to every aliasing identity fast path.
      ⍝
      V←1 2 3 ⋄ (0⌽V)←9 ⋄ V
9 9 9

      V←1 2 3 ⋄ (0⌽V)←9 9
LENGTH ERROR+
      (0⌽V)←9 9
      ^    ^
      )SIC

      V←5 ⋄ (⌽V)←9 9
LENGTH ERROR+
      (⌽V)←9 9
      ^   ^
      )SIC

      M←2 3⍴⍳6 ⋄ (1 2⍉M)←9 ⋄ M
9 9 9
9 9 9

