      ⍝ Regression: Bugs25 #2 (Blake McBride). A float or complex literal
      ⍝ in [ ] (axis or index) was rejected at parse time with a bare
      ⍝ DOMAIN ERROR, even when the equivalent variable works fine --
      ⍝ Parser::optimize_literal_axes() called Cell::get_int_value()
      ⍝ (IntCell-only) instead of Cell::get_near_int() (any near-int
      ⍝ numeric cell) on a literal already established as near-int.
      ⍝ Fixed in Parser.cc; a near-int literal too large to fit an
      ⍝ int64_t (e.g. 1E30) must still not be taken down the sAxis fast
      ⍝ path (Parser.cc), and must not crash Token::canonical() when
      ⍝ later rendering the caret line for the resulting runtime error
      ⍝ (Token.cc -- get_near_int() there can throw too, and naively
      ⍝ catching that throw does not help, since Error::update_error_info()
      ⍝ recurses through plain calls, not exception unwinding).
      ⍝
      ⍝ Bugs28 #100(t): an index is by definition a ShapeItem (int64_t);
      ⍝ cValue::index(cValue_R) used to call the throwing get_near_int()
      ⍝ unconditionally, so a too-large index (like 1E30 below) raised a
      ⍝ bare, uncontextualized DOMAIN ERROR instead of the well-formed
      ⍝ INDEX ERROR it actually is. Fixed by checking is_near_int64_t()
      ⍝ (which cannot itself throw) first. Token.cc's caret-line
      ⍝ rendering (above, right below "must not crash Token::canonical()")
      ⍝ used to fall back to a meaningless "?" for the very same
      ⍝ too-large-for-int64_t values, for the very same reason
      ⍝ (get_near_int() throws); it now instead formats the value safely
      ⍝ via get_real_value() (which does not throw for a numeric cell),
      ⍝ so the caret line below shows 1E30, not [?].
      ⍝
      ⌽[2.0](2 2⍴⍳4)
2 1
4 3

      (1 2 3)[2.0]
2

      (1 2 3)[1J0]
1

      1↑[1J0](2 2⍴⍳4)
1 2

      ⌽[1E10](2 2⍴⍳4)
AXIS ERROR+
      ⌽[10000000000](2 2⍴⍳4)
      ^

      )SIC

      (1 2 3)[1E30]
INDEX ERROR+
      (1 2 3)[1E30]
      ^      ^

      )MORE
A[B]: B is not a valid index for A (an integer too large for a (signed) 63-bit index)
      )SIC

      ⎕FX 'Z←FOO' 'Z←(1 2 3)[1E30]'
FOO

      FOO
INDEX ERROR+
FOO[1]  Z←(1 2 3)[1E30]
          ^      ^

      )MORE
A[B]: B is not a valid index for A (an integer too large for a (signed) 63-bit index)
      )SIC

      ⎕EX 'FOO'
1



